### What are the JVM memory areas?

The JVM divides memory into several distinct regions, each serving a specific purpose. Understanding them is essential for debugging production issues like OutOfMemoryError, StackOverflowError, and high GC pause times.

```java
package com.bank.jvm;

public class MemoryAreasDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Heap: objects created with new
        Object account = new Object();  // lives on the heap

        // Stack: local variables and method frames
        int balance = 50000;  // primitive on the stack

        // Metaspace: class metadata loaded when this class first runs
        System.out.println("Heap max: " + Runtime.getRuntime().maxMemory() / (1024 * 1024) + " MB");
        System.out.println("Heap used: " + (Runtime.getRuntime().totalMemory() - Runtime.getRuntime().freeMemory()) / (1024 * 1024) + " MB");
    }
}
```

```text
Heap max: 256 MB
Heap used: 5 MB
```

### How does the heap work with generational collection?

The heap is split into generations because most objects die young. The Young Generation has an Eden space plus two Survivor spaces (S0 and S1). New objects are allocated in Eden. When Eden fills up, a Minor GC runs, copying surviving objects into a Survivor space. Objects that survive several minor collections are promoted to the Old Generation, which holds long-lived objects.

```java
package com.bank.jvm;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class GenerationalDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Short-lived objects: created in Eden, collected in Minor GC
        for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
            BigDecimal fee = new BigDecimal("10.50").multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(i));
            // fee is unreachable after this iteration -> collected in Minor GC
        }

        // Long-lived object: survives many GC cycles -> promoted to Old Gen
        List<String> accountCache = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 10_000; i++) {
            accountCache.add("ACC" + i);
        }
        System.out.println("Cache size: " + accountCache.size());
    }
}
```

```bash
java -Xlog:gc* -Xmx128m com.bank.jvm.GenerationalDemo
```

```text
[GC (Allocation Failure) Young: 33792K->4096K  Pause: 5ms]
[GC (Allocation Failure) Young: 37888K->4096K  Pause: 4ms]
Cache size: 10000
```

### How does the stack work?

Each thread has its own stack. When a method is called, a new frame is pushed onto the stack holding local variables, the operand stack, and the return address. When the method returns, the frame is popped. Primitives and object references live on the stack, but the objects they point to live on the heap.

```java
package com.bank.jvm;

public class StackDemo {

    static void transfer(String from, String to, int amount) {
        // Frame for transfer() pushed onto stack
        debit(from, amount);
        credit(to, amount);
        // Frame for transfer() popped when returning
    }

    static void debit(String account, int amount) {
        System.out.println("Debit " + amount + " from " + account);
    }

    static void credit(String account, int amount) {
        System.out.println("Credit " + amount + " to " + account);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        transfer("ACC1001", "ACC2001", 5000);
    }
}
```

```text
Debit 5000 from ACC1001
Credit 5000 to ACC2001
```

### What causes StackOverflowError?

Infinite or excessively deep recursion pushes more frames than the stack can hold, causing `StackOverflowError`. The default stack size is around 512 KB to 1 MB per thread. You can increase it with `-Xss`, but the real fix is usually to convert recursion to iteration.

```java
package com.bank.jvm;

public class StackOverflowDemo {

    static int calculateCompoundInterest(int months) {
        if (months == 0) return 0;
        return calculateCompoundInterest(months - 1) + 100;  // no base case for negative
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            calculateCompoundInterest(100_000);  // too deep
        } catch (StackOverflowError e) {
            System.out.println("StackOverflowError: recursion too deep");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
StackOverflowError: recursion too deep
```

### How does the JIT compiler make Java fast?

The JVM first interprets bytecode. When it detects hot methods that run thousands of times, it compiles them to native code using tiered compilation. The C1 compiler does a quick compile with basic optimizations. The C2 compiler does heavy optimization for the hottest methods, including method inlining, escape analysis, and dead code elimination.

```java
package com.bank.jvm;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class JitDemo {

    static BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount, BigDecimal rate) {
        return amount.multiply(rate);  // hot method, will be JIT-compiled
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal rate = new BigDecimal("0.015");

        // Warm up: JIT compiles after ~10,000 invocations
        for (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) {
            calculateFee(BigDecimal.valueOf(i), rate);
        }

        // Now the method is JIT-compiled, running at near-native speed
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
            calculateFee(BigDecimal.valueOf(i), rate);
        }
        long elapsed = System.nanoTime() - start;
        System.out.println("1M fee calcs in " + (elapsed / 1_000_000) + " ms");
    }
}
```

```bash
java -XX:+PrintCompilation com.bank.jvm.JitDemo
```

```text
 1       3       com.bank.jvm.JitDemo::calculateFee (C1)
 2       4       com.bank.jvm.JitDemo::calculateFee (C2)
1M fee calcs in 45 ms
```

### How do you analyze thread dumps?

`jstack <pid>` prints every thread's state. You look for BLOCKED or WAITING threads to find deadlocks, and for threads stuck in the same method across multiple dumps to find contention.

```bash
# Capture thread dump of a running process
jstack $(pgrep -f BankingApp) > thread-dump.txt

# Look for deadlocks
grep -A 5 "deadlock" thread-dump.txt

# Look for blocked threads
grep -B 2 "BLOCKED" thread-dump.txt
```

